>      If you're using some flavor of Unix, including linux, it's
> trivial.  If not, you need an implementation of Unix-style `diff'
> (which probably exists for every platform nethack runs on, certainly
> for every one which has an implementation of `patch'; if you're using
> GNU tools, diff is part of the Diffutils package).  Then use
>
> diff -u original_file modified_file > patch_file
>
> to produce sets of line changes with "the header stuff" automatically
> generated.  (Old habits die hard; I prefer `diff -c' over `diff -u').

Also, it is helpful to maintain a completely clean "vanilla" directory
next to the directory in which you're modifying files; then from the
level above both directories, you can use a command like:

diff -Nurd nethack nh_changed >mypatch.diff

and this will recurse down into each, find all your changes, and park
them all nicely into "mypatch.diff".  This is the method I use to
generate Sporkhack's patches, and so far it seems to work quite well;
the recipient simply needs to execute:

patch -p1 <mypatch.diff

in their Nethack distro directory, and presto.

